# =============================================================================
# Frontend environment template - Used Car Marketplace web client
# =============================================================================
#
# Copy this file to frontend/.env and replace both placeholder values:
#
#     cp frontend/.env.example frontend/.env
#
# frontend/.env is git-ignored (see .gitignore) and must stay that way. This
# template is committed, so NOTHING IN IT IS A SECRET AND NOTHING IN IT SHOULD
# BE - and that is a stronger statement here than it is for the backend. Every
# value below is substituted into the JavaScript bundle as a literal by the
# `define` block in vite.config.ts, which means it is readable by anybody who
# opens the shipped file. There is no such thing as a confidential frontend
# environment variable. Anything that must stay secret belongs in backend/.env,
# where it is read by a process the browser never sees.
#
# WHERE IT IS READ. Vite's `loadEnv` reads frontend/.env, resolved against the
# PROCESS working directory, so the commands below have to be run from
# frontend/:
#
#     cd frontend && npx vite --host 127.0.0.1 --port 3000   # dev server
#     cd frontend && npm run build                           # tsc && vite build
#
# WHEN IT IS READ. vite.config.ts validates these values while the config is
# being loaded, before a single module is transformed - so a wrong value fails
# the build or the dev server immediately, with a message naming the variable,
# instead of producing a bundle that is broken only once a browser runs it.
#
# WHICH VARIABLES EXIST. Exactly the two below. vite.config.ts inlines an
# explicit allow-list (`KNOWN_ENV_KEYS`), not every `REACT_APP_`-prefixed
# variable it can find, so adding a third variable takes two edits in the same
# change: the `process.env` reader, and that list. A variable set here but not
# listed there is ignored, and the config says so on stderr when it happens.
#
# WHAT IS NOT NEEDED. Neither variable is required to run the test suite.
# `vitest` loads this config with mode "test", and the requirement is enforced
# only for the "development" and "production" modes that produce something a
# browser runs, so `npx vitest run` and `npm run lint` work in a fresh checkout
# with no .env file at all. `npm run build` and the dev server do need both.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED to build or serve - both, with no defaults
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Base URL of the backend API, including the /api prefix.
#
# Read by src/services/api.ts and src/services/rating.ts, which build their
# axios instances from it; every request path is APPENDED to this value, so all
# five /api/ratings endpoints plus listings, transactions and messages are
# addressed relative to it.
#
# vite.config.ts refuses, with a message naming the problem:
#   - an empty or unset value in development/production
#   - a relative value: the URL must be absolute and include its scheme
#   - a scheme other than http or https
#   - embedded credentials (user:pass@host) - they would be published in the
#     bundle, and browsers ignore userinfo on fetch requests in any case
#   - a query string or fragment - appended paths would land in the middle of
#     the URL, producing 404s from a server that is working perfectly
#   - a path that does not end in /api - the backend mounts every router there
#
# Use the value below verbatim against a locally running backend
# (cd backend && python -m uvicorn app.main:app --reload --port 8000).
REACT_APP_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000/api

# Stripe PUBLISHABLE key - pk_test_… for development, pk_live_… in production.
#
# Read by src/index.tsx, which passes it straight to loadStripe() at module
# scope, and by src/services/payment.ts. The placeholder below is not a real
# key: replace it with the publishable key from your own Stripe dashboard
# (Developers -> API keys), or payment initialisation will fail in the browser.
#
# vite.config.ts refuses a value beginning `sk_` or `rk_` IN EVERY MODE,
# including test. Those prefixes denote a secret and a restricted key, and
# either one placed here is disclosed to every visitor to the site. The
# publishable key is designed for exactly this exposure; the others are not.
# Secret keys belong in backend/.env as STRIPE_API_KEY, which is read only by
# the API process.
REACT_APP_STRIPE_PUBLIC_KEY=pk_test_replace_with_your_publishable_key


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT SETTINGS - listed so their absence is not read as an omission
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The backend's own configuration is a separate file with a separate template:
# see backend/.env.example. Its eight required values - including SECRET_KEY,
# STRIPE_API_KEY and STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET - must never be copied here.
#
# There is no NODE_ENV entry. Vite derives the mode from the command (`vite`
# gives "development", `vite build` gives "production", `vitest` gives "test")
# and setting NODE_ENV by hand only disagrees with it.
#
# There is no PORT entry. The dev server port is fixed in vite.config.ts
# (`server.port`, 3000) so that it matches the origin the backend's
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS default permits; changing one without the other breaks CORS.
